  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 153 PM CDT Sun Aug 30 2026\n\nAnother hot afternoon across the area, with an upper high sitting to \nour south over Oklahoma. A few showers are still lingering just \nnorthwest of Republic County, keeping a few clouds around there, but \nelsewhere it is just hot and windy. Dewpoints are running a degree \nor two yesterday's values, and temperatures several degrees above. \nThis should take temperatures around or above 100 with another hour \nor two of heating. As most places saw heat index values in the 102 \nto 105 range yesterday, more confident that most places will see \nheat indices around or above 105 today. So have gone ahead and \nissued a Heat Advisory for the entire area today and tomorrow, with \nthe exception of counties along the Nebraska border where cloud \ncover may keep temperatures a few degrees cooler. Tuesday through \nthe rest of the week, the upper high moves a bit farther east but \nstays the dominant feature across eastern KS. Confidence is high in \ncontinued high temperatures in the upper 90s through Friday, with \nheat indices around or above 100 each day. So may an extension of \nthe Heat Advisory, particularly if dewpoints can creep up a few \ndegrees by mid-week. As mentioned yesterday, persistent southerly \nwinds will be a double-edged sword, slightly improving the feel of \nthe air but also not allowing temperatures to fall as much \novernight. Low temperatures will continue to be in the upper 70s \neach day.\n\nAside from the heat, the only thing to watch over the next week will \nbe some very low rain chances, mainly over far north-central KS. \nSimilar to this morning, a weak boundary will stretch from northwest \nKansas into south-central Nebraska tonight. As the nocturnal LLJ \nrestrengthens, a few more showers and weak thunderstorms should \ndevelop to our west near the boundary. These may move into Republic \nCounty and nearby areas for a few hours tomorrow morning. While the \nboundary should move off a bit to the northwest Tuesday and \nWednesday, by Thursday and Friday mornings it may move back close \nenough to allow for some more isolated AM convection. Regardless, \nexpecting any rainfall that does occur through the week to stay very\nlight, and mainly confined to the Nebraska border.",
